# Getting started with SWI-Prolog

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:swi_elixir, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "== 0.19.0"}
])
```

## Introduction

This notebook introduces **the Elixir wrapper**: starting a session, asking
questions, reading values, loading a program, and releasing resources.
[SWI-Prolog](https://github.com/SWI-Prolog/swipl-devel) parses the source and
performs unification and search. Elixir orchestrates the work and displays
its results.

Evaluate the notebook from top to bottom in a local Livebook runtime. Setup
installs this checkout, its bundled SWI runtime, and Kino for tables and
diagrams. On a [platform with an available bundle](../README.md#platforms-and-native-bundles), no separate SWI installation or compiler
is needed. The initial Kino installation needs network access; examples use local data.

For a tour of proof search, constraint programming, and program learning,
start with the [tutorial chapters](intro.livemd).

* [Start a session](#preliminaries-start-a-session)
* [Read results](#read-results-as-elixir-values)
* [Load and query a program](#load-a-small-program)
* [Control a lazy search](#stop-a-search-early)
* [Handle errors](#handle-errors)

## Preliminaries: start a session

`SWI` is the public API for both backends. Choose `:port` for a separate SWI
process or `:nif` for embedded SWI. The functions and returned values are the
same. Change this setting before evaluating the workflow from the beginning.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
backend = :port
{:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)
```

Keep this session across cells so later queries can use loaded predicates.
Before reevaluating a session-creation cell, close the previous session using
the final cell. Restarting the notebook runtime also releases its sessions.

## Ask a question

Use `call/3` when you need to know whether a goal has a solution. The goal is
ordinary Prolog text, with an optional final period.

```elixir
{:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "member(b,[a,b,c])")
{:ok, false} = SWI.call(session, "member(z,[a,b,c])")
```

Both calls ran successfully. `false` means the search found no solution;
parsing and execution errors use `{:error, reason}` instead.

## Read results as Elixir values

`query/3` returns a lazy enumerable of binding maps. A map's keys are variable
names without Prolog punctuation. Enumerating asks SWI for successive answers.

```elixir
bindings = SWI.query(session, "X=f(a,[1,2]), Y=Z") |> Enum.to_list()
[answer] = bindings
assert answer["X"] == {:compound, "f", [{:atom, "a"}, [1, 2]]}
assert answer["Y"] == answer["Z"]
bindings
```

Atoms and functor names are binaries inside tagged tuples, so a query cannot
fill the BEAM atom table. The equal `{:var, id}` values for `Y` and `Z` describe
the same unbound variable. IDs only have meaning within that answer.

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(
  for({name, value} <- answer, do: %{variable: name, value: inspect(value)}),
  keys: [:variable, :value]
)
```

| Prolog value            | Elixir value                       |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `hello`                 | `{:atom, "hello"}`                 |
| `42`, `3.5`             | integer, float                     |
| `"hello"`               | binary                             |
| `[a,1]`                 | `[{:atom, "a"}, 1]`                |
| `f(a)`                  | `{:compound, "f", [{:atom, "a"}]}` |
| unbound variable        | `{:var, id}`                       |
| extended or cyclic term | `{:prolog, rendered_text}`         |

## Load a small program

Our application needs to follow a chain of parent relationships. This is the
complete program: `parent/2` supplies the edges, and `ancestor/2` follows one
or more edges. The module keeps these names separate from other examples.

```elixir
family = ~S"""
:- module(family, [parent/2, ancestor/2]).
:- dynamic parent/2.
parent(ada, grace).
parent(grace, alan).
parent(alan, emmy).
ancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Y).
ancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Z), ancestor(Z, Y).
"""
```

`load/3` takes a filename. We create a temporary file from the visible source
and remove it after loading; the predicates stay available in the session.
The stable filename allows SWI to reconsult the module when you rerun the cell.

```elixir
path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "swi-livebook-family-#{System.pid()}.pl")
File.write!(path, family)

try do
  :ok = SWI.load(session, path)
after
  File.rm!(path)
end
```

## Query the program

Predict Ada's descendants before evaluating the next cell.

```elixir
descendants = SWI.query(session, "family:ancestor(ada, Who)") |> Enum.to_list()

assert Enum.map(descendants, & &1["Who"]) == [
         {:atom, "grace"},
         {:atom, "alan"},
         {:atom, "emmy"}
       ]

Kino.DataTable.new(for %{"Who" => {:atom, name}} <- descendants, do: %{descendant: name})
```

The diagram below is built from **queried parent facts**, rather than a second
copy of the dataset. Changing those facts changes the displayed graph.

```elixir
edges = SWI.query(session, "family:parent(Parent, Child)") |> Enum.to_list()

diagram =
  "flowchart LR\n" <>
    Enum.map_join(edges, "\n", fn edge ->
      {:atom, parent} = edge["Parent"]
      {:atom, child} = edge["Child"]
      "  #{parent} --> #{child}"
    end)

Kino.Mermaid.new(diagram)
```

## Change state and query again

The session retains loaded code and dynamic facts. Add one edge and ask the
same question again. `descendants` remains the earlier Elixir snapshot.

```elixir
{:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "assertz(family:parent(emmy,nora))")
updated = SWI.query(session, "family:ancestor(ada, Who)") |> Enum.to_list()
assert length(updated) == 4
assert length(descendants) == 3
Kino.DataTable.new(for %{"Who" => {:atom, name}} <- updated, do: %{descendant: name})
```

Reevaluating `assertz/1` adds another fact. Reload the program cell to restore
its original facts before repeating the full workflow. Source loading can
partially change code or state before reporting an error; it is not a
transaction.

### Try it yourself

Ask `family:ancestor(Who,emmy)` in a new query cell. Predict the names before
evaluating, then compare the bindings with the diagram.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Ada, Grace, and Alan are ancestors of Emmy. Emmy is not her own ancestor in
this acyclic program, and adding a child of Emmy does not add an ancestor of her.

</details>

## Stop a search early

SWI retains the query's choice points while Elixir consumes answers.
`Enum.take/2` closes the remaining search so the session can immediately be
used again. Use a separate session for each concurrent stream.

```elixir
first_three = SWI.query(session, "between(1,inf,N)") |> Enum.take(3)
assert Enum.map(first_three, & &1["N"]) == [1, 2, 3]
{:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "true")
Kino.DataTable.new(first_three)
```

## Handle errors

An exception from Prolog becomes a tagged error for `call/3`. An error during
stream enumeration raises `SWI.Error` with the same reason.

```elixir
{:error, {:prolog, "oops"}} = SWI.call(session, "throw(oops)")

stream_error =
  try do
    SWI.query(session, "throw(oops)") |> Enum.to_list()
  rescue
    error in SWI.Error -> error.reason
  end

assert stream_error == {:prolog, "oops"}
{:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "true")
stream_error
```

The default timeout is five seconds per solution pull, excluding time spent
consuming answers. Use the backend chapter for timeout and recovery examples.

## Choose a backend through the same API

The independent operation below runs with both backends. Each session closes
in an `after` block, including when a query or assertion raises.

```elixir
answers =
  for selected <- [:port, :nif], into: %{} do
    {:ok, other} = SWI.start_link(backend: selected)

    try do
      {selected, SWI.query(other, "member(X,[a,b,c])") |> Enum.to_list()}
    after
      SWI.stop(other)
    end
  end

assert answers.port == answers.nif
Kino.DataTable.new(answers.port)
```

Port has a separate database per session. NIF sessions have independent query
engines but share ordinary SWI modules and dynamic predicates within the BEAM
VM. Coordinate loading and mutations when using the embedded backend.

## Close the session

Release the interactive session when finished. Values already returned to
Elixir remain usable. To revisit the lesson, evaluate from the beginning.

```elixir
:ok = SWI.stop(session)
```

